<Docs title="Maintenance & Recovery" description="Diagnose, deny, quarantine, and verify Omegon without starting the normal runtime.">
  <h1>Maintenance &amp; Recovery</h1>
  <p>
    <code>omegon-maintain</code> is the release-coupled recovery companion. It remains
    independently runnable when the normal TUI, agent loop, provider setup, project
    configuration, plugins, extensions, MCP, mutable packs, memory, lifecycle, or
    orchestration state is broken.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    It is a bounded data-inspection and recovery tool, not a second agent harness,
    generic repair shell, package manager, or mode of <code>omegon</code>.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>Start With Read-Only Evidence</h2>
  <pre><code>{snippet("maintenance.identity")}
{snippet("maintenance.doctor")}
{snippet("maintenance.composition_inspect")}
{snippet("maintenance.release_inspect")}</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li><code>identity</code> reports the artifact, protocol, limits, target, and compiled exclusions without loading normal runtime roots.</li>
    <li><code>composition inspect</code> reports the compiled maintenance profile, not a live contribution graph.</li>
    <li><code>doctor</code> runs bounded structural checks; unavailable evidence can produce a degraded result.</li>
    <li><code>release inspect</code> inspects adjacent package metadata only. Use offline verification for signed release evidence.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Stable Automation Contract</h2>
  <p>
    With <code>--json</code>, normal termination writes exactly one JSON object to stdout;
    logs and progress go to stderr. Status is <code>success</code>, <code>failure</code>, or
    <code>degraded</code>.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Exit</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>All requested work settled successfully.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>Definite failure, refusal, invalid input, unsupported operation, or timeout before dispatch.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>2</code></td><td>Degraded or partial evidence, possible-dispatch timeout, unknown settlement, or audit/output settlement failure.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Do not treat exit <code>2</code> as success or blindly retry a mutation. Reconciliation
    may reuse the same request UUID only for the exact same command fingerprint.
  </p>

  <h2>Roots, Deadlines, and Dry Runs</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>--workspace</code>, <code>--home</code>, and <code>--config-home</code> require existing absolute directories.</li>
    <li>Project contribution, selected-session, and resource operations require an explicit <code>--workspace</code>.</li>
    <li>User contribution listing uses <code>--scope user</code> without <code>--workspace</code>.</li>
    <li>Every mutation and mutation dry run requires <code>--deadline</code>. Durations use an unsigned integer plus <code>ms</code>, <code>s</code>, or <code>m</code>, with a ten-minute maximum.</li>
    <li>Final root symlinks, aliased roots, and group/other-writable mutation roots are rejected.</li>
  </ul>
  <blockquote>
    <code>--dry-run</code> does not dispatch the requested mutation, but it may bootstrap
    maintenance-owned state, create or acquire lock files, and append a dry-run audit record.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>Contributions</h2>
  <pre><code>{snippet("maintenance.contribution_list_project")}

{snippet("maintenance.contribution_inspect_project")}

{snippet("maintenance.contribution_disable_dry_run")}

{snippet("maintenance.contribution_disable")}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Selectors use <code>&lt;kind&gt;:&lt;id&gt;</code>, where kind is
    <code>extension</code>, <code>plugin</code>, <code>skill</code>, <code>prompt</code>,
    <code>catalog</code>, or <code>workflow</code>. Listing gives invalid filesystem names an
    opaque <code>entry:sha256:&lt;hex&gt;</code> selector instead of interpreting them. IDs match
    <code>[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]{0,127}</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Disable affects future activation; it does not stop a running process. Quarantine first
    settles the deny, then atomically moves a real entry on the same filesystem or unlinks a
    contribution symlink without opening or modifying its target:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{snippet("maintenance.contribution_quarantine")}</code></pre>
  <p>Restore, enable, purge, recursive deletion, and copy-delete fallback are not available.</p>

  <h2>Sessions</h2>
  <pre><code>{snippet("maintenance.session_list")}

{snippet("maintenance.session_inspect")}

{snippet("maintenance.session_quarantine_dry_run")}

{snippet("maintenance.session_quarantine")}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Selection requires one exact session and workspace identity. Quarantine installs resume-deny
    authority while preserving session and metadata bytes. It does not rewrite snapshots,
    synthesize events, change completion state, or semantically repair a conversation.
  </p>

  <h2>Invocation Mutation Fences</h2>
  <p>
    If a mutating invocation may have reached its owner but acknowledgement or settlement cannot be
    made durable, Omegon records independent emergency fence evidence and denies later mutations with
    the same declared domain and key. This is an unknown-settlement safety condition, not an ordinary
    tool failure. Do not delete or edit fence files and do not blindly retry the mutation.
  </p>
  <p>
    The current <code>omegon-maintain</code> release does not inspect, reconcile, or clear runtime
    invocation fences. Clearing requires deterministic owner reconciliation or a future explicit,
    audited operator recovery action; session quarantine does not clear these records.
  </p>

  <h2>Runtime Ownership Records</h2>
  <pre><code>{snippet("maintenance.resource_list")}

{snippet("maintenance.resource_prune_dry_run")}

{snippet("maintenance.resource_prune")}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Pruning removes only records proven stale by heartbeat, boot, PID, and process-start evidence.
    It never kills a process. Legacy, malformed, clock-skewed, or incomplete records remain
    inspect-only or unverifiable.
  </p>

  <h2>Audit Evidence</h2>
  <pre><code>{snippet("maintenance.audit_inspect")}
{snippet("maintenance.audit_verify")}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Audit verification checks structural hash-chain continuity. It is not authenticity proof
    against an attacker who can rewrite all user-owned maintenance state.
  </p>

  <h2>Offline Release Verification</h2>
  <pre><code>{snippet("verify.release_verify")}</code></pre>
  <p>
    All operands must be absolute regular files. Verification performs no discovery or download,
    and streams the archive without extracting or executing it. Compiled policy checks repository,
    workflow, issuer, and tag identity while the signed manifest binds the archive digest,
    provenance, target, version, commit, and both executable identities.
  </p>

  <h2>Not Implemented</h2>
  <p>
    There are no <code>fix</code>, <code>repair</code>, <code>clean</code>, <code>update</code>, or
    <code>rollback</code> aliases. Maintenance does not edit project source/configuration, run
    hooks or probes, kill processes, repair conversation semantics, or discover, download,
    install, activate, switch, or roll back releases.
  </p>
</Docs>
